                <td><span class="badge badge-orange">Parity Layer</span></td>
                <td>JSON Diffing, Custom Normalizer</td>
                <td><strong>Economic Validator:</strong> Normalizes cell formats (e.g., standardizing <code>.</code> vs <code>N/A</code>, space stripping). Audits tabular datasets column-by-column and alerts on mismatch.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td class="bold-lead">Report Compiler<br><span style="font-family: Courier New; font-size:11px;">src/pom/<br>ReportCompiler.js</span></td>
                <td><span class="badge badge-teal">Reporting</span></td>
                <td>HTML5, CSS3 Grid, Tailwind, active_run.json</td>
                <td><strong>Registry Guardian &amp; Builder:</strong> Generates unified dashboards. Preserves worker logs across crashes using <code>process.ppid</code> state locking. Renders row-by-row comparative charts.</td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <!-- Section 4: Advanced Engineering Solutions -->
    <h2>4. Advanced Engineering Solutions (The "Wow" Factors)</h2>
    <p>
        The framework goes far beyond standard visual/functional automation. It incorporates professional-grade engineering workarounds designed to combat framework-specific timing, layout, and state-binding bugs:
    </p>

    <div class="highlight-box purple">
        <strong style="color: #5b21b6;">⚡ Challenge 1: GWT Invisible Input States (Bypassing Simple Binding)</strong><br>
        <em>Problem:</em> Simply changing an input element's <code>value</code> in GWT leaves GWT's underlying state bindings empty, leading to blank searches.<br>
        <em>Solution:</em> We built a bubble event chain that simulates realistic mouse-and-keyboard interactions. Inside the nested iframe, we sequentially dispatch four separate events on the DOM node: <code>Focus &rarr; Input &rarr; Change &rarr; Blur</code>. This triggers GWT's internal framework handlers, syncing the programmatic entry instantly.
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box amber">
        <strong style="color: #b45309;">⚡ Challenge 2: Split-Pane Coordinate Grid Merging</strong><br>
        <em>Problem:</em> GWT displays grid records split across a left-frozen table container (containing status icons) and a floating right table container (containing trade economics). Because these are independent HTML tables, cell elements do not align under a single columns tag.<br>
        <em>Solution:</em> We designed a coordinate-based merging algorithm. The tool matches table rows 1-to-1 by index, and maps cell values to grid headers dynamically by computing the absolute horizontal midpoint distance:
        <div style="text-align: center; font-weight: bold; margin: 10px 0; color: #78350f;">
            midpoint = (rect.left + rect.right) / 2
        </div>
        It dynamically pairs the cell to the closest header if it lies within a <strong>100px tolerance proximity threshold</strong>.
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box">
        <strong style="color: #1e3a8a;">⚡ Challenge 3: Playwright Worker-Respawn Registry Wipe (The Session Lock)</strong><br>
        <em>Problem:</em> Playwright Test runs test cases in parallel using isolated workers. If a test fails, Playwright tears down that worker and spawns a brand new one to prevent side-effects. This fresh worker re-runs <code>beforeAll</code> hooks, which originally wiped the central reporting database, dividing a single test run into disjointed, incomplete reports.<br>
        <em>Solution:</em> We engineered a persistent registry lock using the <strong>Parent Process ID (process.ppid)</strong>. All worker processes share the exact same parent process ID. We store the parent PID inside a lightweight lockfile (<code>active_run.json</code>). When a new worker spawns, it reads the file. If the parent PID matches, it bypasses database initialization, preserves completed test results, and appends the new status, compiling all cases into a single, unified HTML report.
    </div>

    <div class="highlight-box emerald">
        <strong style="color: #065f46;">⚡ Challenge 4: Overlapping Overlays (Unblocking Screenshots)</strong><br>
        <em>Problem:</em> When date ranges or dropdown filters are set inside PrimeNG inputs, calendar overlays remain expanded. Capturing screenshots at this instant records a huge floating date-picker box blocking the input labels and neighboring checkboxes, ruining the visual comparison layout reports.<br>
        <em>Solution:</em> We created a custom <code>AgGridBlotterPage.dismissOverlays()</code> method. Before any screenshot is initiated, this method programmatically clicks a safe blank sector of the workspace and forces a stable 1000ms delay to allow CSS fade-out animations to settle, capturing pristine, unblocked criteria views.
    </div>

    <!-- Section 5: Presentation Talking Points -->
    <div class="talking-points-card">
        <h3 class="talking-points-title">💬 Presentation Guide: Your "Cheat Sheet" for Technical Leadership</h3>
        <p style="margin-top: 0; color: #5b21b6; font-size: 14px;">
            Use these structured talking points to explain the diagram and architecture to your manager or technical leads with high authority:
        </p>
        
        <div class="talking-point-item">
            <strong>"The Single Source of Truth" (Layer 1):</strong><br>
            "We have aligned our testing entirely around a single Gherkin Markdown document (<code>COMPREHENSIVE_BDD_TEST_CASES.md</code>). Since this is written in plain English, business analysts and non-technical stakeholders can read, write, and audit our test scenarios. Our framework parses this file on-the-fly, meaning a simple edit in plain English instantly alters the automated tests. No code changes are required to add test cases."
        </div>

        <div class="talking-point-item">
            <strong>"Robust Browser Modeling" (Layers 2 &amp; 3):</strong><br>
            "Legacy systems are notoriously unstable during automation because they lack modern DOM bindings. We avoided fragile hardcoded timeouts or raw coordinate clicks. Instead, we developed fully isolated Page Object Models (POM). Our old-UI POM resolves nested GWT iframes, dispatches native bubble event-chains to bind inputs, and stitches coordinate-based split grids. Our new-UI POM auto-expands collapsing search cards and dismisses active calendar overlays."
        </div>

        <div class="talking-point-item">
            <strong>"High-Fidelity Comparative Engine" (Layer 4):</strong><br>
            "We run a two-fold comparison. First, we perform <strong>Data Parity Audits</strong> by scraping raw values from both systems, normalising inconsistencies (such as <code>.</code> vs <code>N/A</code>, dates, and whitespace), and matching them cell-by-cell. Second, we perform <strong>Visual Parity Audits</strong> using Playwright's native screenshot engine with <strong>Custom Masking</strong>. We black out dynamic grid rows to verify the container borders, card headers, and button layouts, completely preventing false pixel-diff failures due to live data changes."
        </div>

        <div class="talking-point-item">
            <strong>"Resilient Session Management" (Layer 5):</strong><br>
            "Playwright runs tests in parallel. To survive worker process crashes and restarts, we implemented a custom registry state-lock utilizing the Parent Process ID (PPID). This ensures that even if a worker crashes, the central test compiler does not wipe the active run's database. It compiles all parallel scenarios, audits, and diagnostic screenshots into a single, interactive, high-end HTML presentation checklist."
        </div>
    </div>

</div>

</body>
</html>
